<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-reverse-triangle-inequality-for-norms" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Reverse triangle inequality for norms
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Prove that
      
      <md>
        \abs{ \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } - \vect{ v }_{ 2 } }
      </md>
      
      for all vectors <m> \vect{ v }_{ 1 } , \vect{ v }_{ 2 } \in V </m> in a normed vector space <m> \of{ V , \norm{ \oparg } } </m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <solution>
    <p>
      The absolute homogeneity and subadditivity of the norm <m> \norm{ \oparg } </m> on <m> V </m> implies that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } \amp \leq \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } - \vect{ v }_{ 2 } + \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } - \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } + \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } = \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } - \vect{ v }_{ 2 } }
        </mrow>
      </md>
      
      and
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } \amp \leq \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } - \vect{ v }_{ 1 } + \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } - \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } + \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } = \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } - \vect{ v }_{ 1 } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \abs{ -1 } \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } - \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } = \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } - \vect{ v }_{ 2 } }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      Thus
      
      <md>
        \abs{ \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } } = \max \of{ \set{ \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } , \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 2 } } - \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } } } } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v }_{ 1 } - \vect{ v }_{ 2 } }
      </md>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
